# Analysis: sample-pca

Status: DRAFT

## Goal
Produce a PCA of the RNA-seq samples from gene counts, coloured by condition.
Show sample labels and the percentage of variance explained by PC1 and PC2.

## Inputs
Resolve both inputs with `_system/resolve_artifact.py --project <project> --assay rnaseq_bulk --consumes counts_gene design` again at execution time; stop if resolution fails. Paths below are relative to the project and are the current native resolutions.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| counts_gene | 01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv |
| design | 01_prepare_samplesheets | 01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv |

The design currently contains six samples: A1–A3 untreated and B1–B3 treated.

## Method
1. After approval, write `scripts/sample_pca.py` and `scripts/submit.sh`. Use Python in `gars-bio`, with pandas, NumPy, scikit-learn and Matplotlib. All numerical work runs in the batch job. Check imports there; stop on a missing dependency without installing anything.
2. Read the resolved tab-separated counts and CSV design. Use `gene_id` as the unique gene identifier and exclude `gene_name` from numerical columns. Require unique, non-empty sample IDs and gene IDs, non-empty condition labels, finite non-negative numeric counts, positive library totals, and exact matching sample sets between counts and design. Reorder count columns to design order; stop on mismatches rather than dropping samples. Preserve fractional length-scaled counts without rounding.
3. Calculate each sample's library total over all genes, then counts per million (CPM = count / library total × 1,000,000). Keep genes with CPM ≥ 1 in at least two samples. Transform retained values with `numpy.log2(CPM + 1)`. This is an exploratory library-size-normalised log-CPM PCA; condition labels are used only for colouring, never for feature selection or fitting.
4. Remove genes with zero variance across samples. Use all remaining genes, with no selection by differential expression. Stop if fewer than two usable genes or fewer than three samples remain. Transpose to samples × genes and run `sklearn.decomposition.PCA(n_components=2, svd_solver="full", whiten=False)`. PCA centres each gene; do not scale genes to unit variance, adjust batches, or remove samples.
5. Plot PC1 against PC2 with Matplotlib, using a fixed condition-to-colour mapping (untreated: #0072B2; treated: #D55E00), a condition legend, sample-ID labels, and axis labels giving explained variance percentages. Save PNG at 300 dpi and a vector PDF.
6. Export sample coordinates with condition, library total and retained sample order; explained variance and variance ratio for each plotted PC; and the gene IDs used. Write a concise methods/QC report recording input resolutions, dimensions before and after filtering, condition counts, package versions, exact parameters and explained variance. Do not infer biological effects or perform significance tests.
7. After successful execution, run `stage03_analysis.py verify` to check declared outputs and record completion through the stage's normal provenance mechanism. Keep inputs read-only and write analysis scripts, logs and results only inside this analysis directory.

